\chapter{Executive Summary}
\section{Current Product State}
LINC One is no longer accurately described as a spiritual-gifts form with a Pastor calendar. The supplied repository is a multi-workspace ministry application with public, participant, Pastor, and delegated-administrator experiences. The current implementation combines a React 19/TypeScript frontend on Netlify, Firebase Authentication, Firebase Realtime Database, a Cloudflare Worker/Hono backend, Brevo transactional email, Backblaze B2 private object storage, Google Maps, and Gemini-backed Bezalel AI workflows.

The source review counted \textbf{211 frontend TypeScript/TSX modules} (approximately \textbf{39,140 lines}), \textbf{46 backend source modules} (approximately \textbf{9,707 lines}), \textbf{16 backend test modules}, and \textbf{167 named automated test cases} across frontend and backend test files. The full named-function appendix records \textbf{981 named source functions and callbacks} identified in the current application code.

\section{Major Functional Domains}
\begin{multicols}{2}\begin{itemize}
\item LINC One public landing and bilingual navigation.
\item Two YAML-defined spiritual/ministry assessments.
\item Public Pastor booking and privacy-safe schedule projection.
\item Authenticated Pastor calendar, availability, unavailability, meeting requests, meeting creation, rescheduling, deletion, and ICS export.
\item Bezalel AI for Pastor calendar assistance and public booking guidance.
\item People Development member grouping, assignments, attachments, personal notes, and recurring group meetings.
\item Identifier-based congregation group portal for assignments and meeting information.
\item Confidential People Notes with strengths/weaknesses, comments, follow-up dates, and deletion workflows.
\item Account-based NextGen QA sessions, question moderation, voting, participant verification, session management, files, and mission map.
\item Residual legacy NextGen identifier/survey/certificate code still present in the repository.
\item Administrator hierarchy, capability allocation, assessment management, carousel management, attendance, and LInC Archives.
\item Tutorial authoring, playback, simulation overlays, target picking, and per-user progress.
\item Brevo notifications, B2 signed upload/download workflows, Firebase server access, and CI/CD automation.
\end{itemize}\end{multicols}

\section{Architecture Status}
The application has moved well beyond the v1.2 ``single migrated email route'' stage. Sensitive calendar, assessment, People Notes, People Development, administration, archives, NextGen QA/files, mission-map, and Bezalel operations now have dedicated Worker APIs. However, the migration is not complete: attendance, landing carousel, tutorial data, participant aggregation, and legacy NextGen modules still include direct browser-side Realtime Database access.

\begin{criticalbox}[-- Current High-Priority Security Findings]
Three source-reviewed boundaries deserve immediate attention: (1) \nolinkurl{/api/v1/people-development/notifications} has no authentication middleware; (2) NextGen file operations are currently available to any Firebase-authenticated account rather than a narrower NextGen/admin capability; and (3) the mission-map route checks only for an authenticated token carrying an email even though repository documentation describes a specific mission-login identity. The temporary public Brevo test endpoint is also still mounted.
\end{criticalbox}

\section{How This Release Differs from 1.3}
Release 1.3 was an architectural assessment. Release 1.4 is a \textbf{maintainer's reference manual}. It expands from major modules into UI behavior, route composition, backend contracts, validation rules, persistence paths, storage lifecycles, authorization boundaries, configuration, tests, and source-level catalogs. Page count is therefore a result of coverage rather than a target.
\chapter{Review Method and Source Coverage}
\section{Reviewed Artifacts}
The review included the entire supplied project tree relevant to application behavior: \nolinkurl{src/}, \nolinkurl{backend/src/}, \nolinkurl{backend/test/}, \nolinkurl{.github/workflows/}, \nolinkurl{src/components/forms/*.yml}, \nolinkurl{documentation/database/*.mmd}, package manifests, Worker configuration, Netlify configuration, and environment examples.

\section{Evidence Classes}
\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Implemented}: directly represented by active source code and route composition in the supplied snapshot.
\item \textbf{Repository-documented}: described in README or ERD files and cross-checked where possible against source.
\item \textbf{Legacy/residual}: source still exists but is not the primary route-level implementation.
\item \textbf{Risk/inference}: a conclusion derived from the visible authorization, storage, or data-flow code; clearly labeled as such.
\end{itemize}

\section{Build Verification Limitation}
The supplied ZIP did not include installed \nolinkurl{node_modules}. The documentation therefore uses source and test inspection as its primary evidence. The CI definitions themselves install dependencies with \nolinkurl{npm ci}, build the frontend, test/type-check the backend, deploy the Worker from main, and run the production NextGen contract smoke test. This release does not claim a fresh local TypeScript build result where dependencies were not installed in the review environment.
