\section{Protected Route Behavior}
The shared \texttt{ProtectedRoute} supports Google sign-in and email/password sign-in/sign-up. It presents a loading state while Firebase authentication and Pastor access are resolving, redirects unauthorized signed-in users when a fallback is supplied, and otherwise renders the protected child. The root \texttt{App} wraps all routes with the internationalization provider and tutorial provider.

\section{Application Bootstrap Residue}
At startup, \texttt{App.tsx} still processes the legacy Google OAuth callback and dynamically initializes EmailJS with a public key. This means the repository retains browser integration scaffolding even though many notification flows have moved to the Worker/Brevo boundary.
\chapter{Public Landing and Navigation}
The landing route is a branded LINC One entry surface built from \nolinkurl{LincOneHero}, \nolinkurl{SpiritualProgramFeature}, \nolinkurl{CommunityCarousel}, and \nolinkurl{FloatingLandingNav}. It supports English/Arabic switching, responsive motion, reduced-motion behavior, footer links, and a scroll-triggered quick-access navigation.

\subsection{Hero and Top-Level Destinations}
The hero exposes the Spiritual Gifts Program as the dominant feature, plus NextGen, About Us, Pastor Login, and Administrator Panel. The floating navigation reproduces the five agreed top-level destinations after the hero action block passes above the viewport.

\subsection{Spiritual Gifts Program Cluster}
The program section groups the assessment, booking, and People Development group-note experiences instead of presenting them as unrelated products. This is reflected by route tab selection in \texttt{App.tsx}: assessment, booking, and group notes all map back to the spiritual-program navigation context.

\subsection{Community Carousel}
\texttt{CommunityCarousel.tsx} subscribes directly to \nolinkurl{landingPage/carousel} in Realtime Database. It normalizes URL, data-URL, and legacy raw Base64 image values. Administrator carousel editing remains a direct-Firebase path in the current frontend.
\chapter{Assessment Platform}
The assessment platform is a reusable YAML-driven engine rather than two hard-coded forms. \texttt{AssessmentForm} composes a selector, questionnaire, results view, and \texttt{useAssessmentForm}. Frontend calculations are implemented in deterministic utilities, while the backend independently validates answers and recomputes persisted results.

\subsection{Assessment Availability}
The public frontend obtains form state from \nolinkurl{/api/v1/assessment/forms}. A form may be \texttt{active}, \texttt{disabled}, or \texttt{hidden}. Hidden forms are not intended for normal selection; disabled forms are visible but unavailable for response acceptance.

\subsection{Submission Trust Boundary}
The browser sends only form ID, locale, and answer values. The backend recognizes only the two configured form IDs, rejects unknown answer keys and invalid rating values, derives the stored fields/scores/results, owns timestamps, persists under \nolinkurl{form}, and then attempts fixed-template Brevo notification. A successful database write is not rolled back merely because email delivery fails.

\subsection{Administrator User Linkage}
Response browsing, identifier linkage, response deletion, identifier email, and form-state changes have moved to administrator endpoints protected by Chief or \texttt{manageAssessmentForms} authority. The public assessment page no longer owns the legacy passcode-based linkage management.
\chapter{Public Booking}
The public booking route renders a calendar/schedule experience and submits requests through the Hono booking API. The backend, not the browser, composes the authoritative schedule by combining availability, unavailability, meetings, pending requests, People Development recurring meetings, and active calendar reservations.

\subsection{Public Schedule Projection}
The schedule endpoint accepts \texttt{start} and \texttt{end} calendar dates and rejects ranges longer than 62 days. Returned data is privacy-safe: visitors receive availability and busy ranges rather than private meeting reasons or participant details.

\subsection{Request Contract}
A booking request requires a 2--100 character name, valid normalized email, date, reason up to 2,000 characters, locale, and an exact 30-minute slot. Slots must fall between 09:00 and 20:00 Toronto time. The schema also includes an empty \texttt{website} honeypot field.

\subsection{Conflict and Reservation Handling}
Before accepting a request, the backend checks that the slot is inside availability and does not overlap unavailability, confirmed meetings, pending requests, recurring group meetings, or another active reservation. A calendar-reservation key is claimed transactionally to reduce double-booking races. The request is persisted as pending and Pastor notification is attempted through Brevo.
\chapter{Pastor Calendar and Meeting Operations}
The Pastor Dashboard consumes a shared authenticated calendar snapshot through \nolinkurl{usePastorCalendarData}. The calendar feature refreshes after mutations and periodically while active. Calendar presentation is separated into hooks/utilities while persistence is provided by the Worker.

\subsection{Calendar Records}
The snapshot spans confirmed meetings, pending meeting requests, availability, unavailability, People Development recurring meeting schedules, and calendar reservation state. UI helpers derive month grids, selected-day content, display titles, acknowledged status, and occurrence labels.

\subsection{Meeting Creation and Editing}
Pastor can create a meeting/event with title, description, date, start/end time, location, meeting type (prayer, counseling, service, other), optional meeting link, and up to 500 participant IDs. Backend validation rejects end time not after start time and checks conflicts before storing.

\subsection{Availability and Unavailability}
Availability opens time for public booking. Unavailability closes time and is checked against meetings, requests, recurring group meetings, and reservations before creation/update. Both support create, edit, and delete paths.

\subsection{Public Request Decisions}
Accepting a request creates the confirmed meeting and advances the reservation/request state; rejecting releases the reservation. Requester notification is generated server-side. Meeting changes can also trigger localized reschedule/cancellation messages.

\subsection{Calendar Export}
The Pastor can export the selected month as an iCalendar \texttt{.ics} file for Apple, Google, Outlook, or another standards-compatible calendar client.
\chapter{Bezalel AI Assistant}
Bezalel is a server-mediated Gemini assistant with two active roles: an authenticated Pastor calendar assistant and a public booking guide. The frontend never receives the Gemini API key.

\subsection{Pastor Assistant}
The Pastor route accepts at most 12 user/assistant messages, each capped at 2,000 characters. It builds a sanitized calendar context containing bounded fields from meetings, requests, availability, unavailability, and recurring group schedules. The Gemini response is constrained to JSON and one of the allowed actions: \texttt{none}, \texttt{open\_availability}, \texttt{block\_time}, \texttt{delete\_availability}, \texttt{delete\_unavailability}, \texttt{accept\_request}, or \texttt{reject\_request}. The frontend executes the chosen action through ordinary authenticated calendar APIs; Gemini does not write the database directly.

\subsection{Public Booking Assistant}
The public assistant receives only the privacy-safe booking projection. It may answer questions, collect visitor information, suggest available slots, and return a \texttt{ready\_to\_book} object. Final booking still passes through the ordinary booking request validation and conflict checks.

\subsection{NextGen AI Roles}
The same server-side Gemini service is used for bilingual QA-theme translation and on-theme question review. Question review accepts reasonable paraphrases and respectful disagreement but rejects spam, unrelated prompts, attacks, and instruction-override attempts. Rejected questions can receive a suggested on-theme rewrite.

\subsection{Provider Contract}
\texttt{generateGeminiJson} uses the configured Gemini model (default \texttt{gemini-3.6-flash}), low temperature, JSON MIME type, a supplied JSON schema, and Zod validation of the returned payload. Missing configuration, rate limits, upstream errors, empty responses, and invalid JSON are mapped into structured Bezalel errors.
\chapter{People Development}
People Development is a Pastor-managed grouping and discipleship workspace backed by \nolinkurl{peopleDevelopment}. It combines member assignment, group-targeted posts, attachments, private member notes, and recurring meeting schedules.

\subsection{Group Taxonomy}
The backend schema recognizes ten group codes: pastors, prophets, evangelists, teachers, apostles, helpers, mercy, facilitators, services, and giving. A member may also temporarily have no assigned group.

\subsection{Member Assignment}
Pastor assignment synchronizes a canonical People Development member record and writes group-linkage metadata back to one or more source assessment records. The source path is constrained and the update map is built by the backend rather than accepting arbitrary database paths from the browser.

\subsection{Assignments and Combined Posts}
Posts may target one or multiple groups, include text up to 20,000 characters, and include up to five attachments. The UI supports group-specific and combined posting, attachment replacement/removal, calendar-style assignment browsing, and deletion.

\subsection{Current Attachment Representation}
People Development attachments are still embedded in Realtime Database as Base64 objects. Each attachment is limited to 1 MiB and the schema caps a post at five attachments. This differs from LInC Archives and NextGen files, which use Backblaze B2 signed URLs.

\subsection{Personal Notes}
Pastor can record member-specific \texttt{strength} or \texttt{weakness} notes with group/member metadata. These are distinct from the separate \texttt{peopleNotes} subsystem.

\subsection{Recurring Group Meetings}
Schedules support group-specific or shared audiences, first/second/third/fourth/last ordinal, weekday 0--6, start time, 30--480 minute duration, start/end dates, and active status. The schedules feed both the Pastor calendar and participant group portal.
\chapter{Congregation Group Notes Portal}
The \nolinkurl{/group-notes} experience is an identifier-based People Development portal. The browser submits only the participant identifier to \nolinkurl{/api/v1/people-development/portal}; the backend resolves the matching member and returns only that member's profile, assignments visible to the assigned group, and active matching/shared schedules.

The portal displays member name, identifier, email, primary gift, group identity/description, assignment count, latest assignment, searchable assignment history, attachment chips, a details dialog with open/download actions, the next group meeting, and the next shared meeting. A recognized member without a group receives an explicit unassigned state rather than another group's data.

The backend adds an in-memory attempt limiter to the identifier endpoint and returns HTTP 429 with \texttt{Retry-After} after the configured threshold. Because Worker instances are distributed and ephemeral, this is a lightweight abuse control rather than a globally durable rate-limit service.
\chapter{Confidential People Notes}
People Notes is a dedicated Pastor-only development-record subsystem separate from People Development personal notes. The route \nolinkurl{/pastor/people-notes} is protected in the frontend and every Worker route independently requires Firebase authentication plus the Pastor allowlist.

A person record may contain nested development items, comments, and follow-up dates. The UI supports creating people, adding development items, adding comments, setting/removing follow-up dates, deleting comments, deleting items, and deleting a person. Backend audit fields and timestamps are server-controlled. Mutations use narrow paths or atomic multipath patches so nested deletes and parent timestamp updates are coordinated.

Responses use private no-store caching. The backend differentiates unavailable configuration, Firebase Rules denial, and upstream database failures in its HTTP mapping.
\chapter{NextGen Account Portal}
The active \nolinkurl{/nextgen-activities} route is now an account-based portal. \texttt{NextGenAuthGateway} supports Firebase email/password account creation and login, with display-name update for new accounts. After authentication the portal exposes three primary sections: QA sessions, NextGen files, and the mission trip map.

\subsection{QA Session Lifecycle}
Managers create sessions with title, description, theme, and status \texttt{draft}, \texttt{open}, or \texttt{closed}. Theme text is translated into English and Arabic by Bezalel. Members can list non-draft sessions; only open sessions permit question submission/voting.

\subsection{Question Submission}
Each account is limited to two submitted questions per QA session. Before persistence, Bezalel compares the question with the bilingual session theme. Off-theme questions return HTTP 422 and are not written. Accepted records have fixed upvote/downvote choices and server-owned metadata.

\subsection{Voting and Privacy}
Votes are keyed so one current choice exists per normalized email identity; a member can change that choice while the session remains open. Public/member views do not expose aggregate vote totals in the ordinary voting UI. Manager views can include participant/voter detail, and aggregate ranking excludes participants marked discarded/unverified according to the backend logic.

\subsection{Management}
Pastor or an administrator with \texttt{manageNextGenQa} can create/update sessions, inspect session detail, verify/discard participants, and select or unselect questions for live discussion.
\chapter{NextGen Files}
NextGen files use an authenticated folder/file workspace backed by Realtime Database metadata and Backblaze B2 object storage. The browser can create nested folders, drag/drop or choose multiple files, refresh state, navigate breadcrumbs, download ready files, delete files, and delete empty folders.

The upload lifecycle is two-phase: create a pending metadata record and signed upload URL, upload the browser file directly to B2, then call the completion endpoint. Completion performs a HEAD/inspection check and rejects a size mismatch before marking the record ready. Downloads are short-lived signed private URLs with a content-disposition filename override.

\begin{warningbox}[-- Current Authorization Scope]
The route-level middleware verifies Firebase authentication globally, but the current file endpoints do not call the QA-manager capability check or another NextGen membership authorization helper. Therefore any authenticated Firebase account can currently list, create, upload, download, and delete NextGen files. This is an implementation boundary, not merely a documentation concern.
\end{warningbox}
\chapter{Montreal Mission Trip Map}
The NextGen mission map renders private locations and connection information using a browser Google Maps key and server-provided location data. The backend stores the map payload in the \texttt{NEXTGEN\_MISSION\_MAP\_DATA} secret, validates location IDs, names, types, addresses, latitude/longitude, notes, parking, languages, and connection references, and returns the result with private/no-store caching.

The frontend supports mission-location cards, map markers, route visualization, Google/Apple Maps links, location-type labels, and travel-distance/time presentation. The code includes both Google-route behavior and fallback distance/duration logic.

\begin{warningbox}[-- Authorization Drift]
Repository README text states that the mission map requires a Firebase password login for a specific NextGen account. The route implementation itself currently authorizes any verified Firebase user whose token contains a non-null email. The source implementation is authoritative for this release and the mismatch is recorded as documentation/security drift.
\end{warningbox}
\chapter{Legacy NextGen Activities Still in Source}
Although the active route uses the account-based portal, the repository still contains the earlier four-character-identifier NextGen activity subsystem under \nolinkurl{src/components/nextgen/activities} and Pastor-side legacy Firebase modules. That subsystem includes registration/approval, four-character IDs, certificate generation, question categories, peer voting, survey responses, participation history, and review logging.

The legacy survey definition contains eight binary preference questions and three rating questions. Browser PDF certificate generation remains implemented in \texttt{nextGenCertificate.ts}. The backend new QA service also contains a one-time legacy migration path that can migrate old QA questions into a closed ``QA Session 1'' and records migration state below \nolinkurl{migrations/legacyQaSession1}.

This code should be treated as compatibility/migration surface until an explicit deletion decision is made; it is not the primary NextGen route-level experience in the supplied snapshot.
\chapter{Administrator Panel}
The Administrator Panel is a dedicated command center rather than a Pastor subpage. Its access hook coordinates Firebase authentication with the backend admin session, pending/suspended states, Chief initialization, authority drafts, activation, suspension, and logout.

\subsection{Admin Areas}
The current feature boundary includes hierarchy/authority management, assessment-form controls, assessment user linkage, carousel management, attendance management, and LInC Archives. An administrator without a required allocation receives a no-authority state rather than silently inheriting access.

\subsection{Chief Bootstrap}
When the admin hierarchy has no Chief and the authenticated user satisfies the exact Pastor allowlist, the backend sets that user's UID as Chief. A newly seen non-Chief account is created in pending status with no authorities. Chief can activate it by saving at least one authority or can suspend an existing administrator.

\subsection{Authority Integrity}
The authority schema requires all five capability booleans and refuses an all-false activation payload. Chief accounts cannot be edited/suspended through the ordinary target-administrator endpoints.
\chapter{Administrator Attendance}
Attendance is currently a frontend-controlled Firebase feature gated by the administrator \texttt{manageAttendance} allocation. The feature is decomposed into people management, recording, analytics, personal analysis, copy/styles, deterministic utilities, and a controller hook.

\subsection{People Records}
Administrators can create/edit people records, search/select people, capture or choose profile photos, switch cameras, remove photos, and persist attendance person data under \nolinkurl{attendance/people}. The normalizer supports URL/data-URL images and legacy raw Base64 photos.

\subsection{Recording}
The recording panel builds Sunday-oriented calendar views, selects an attendance date, and records attendance while avoiding duplicate date entries. Stored attendance is normalized from comma-separated or legacy representations into sorted date lists in the UI.

\subsection{Analytics}
Derived analytics include attendance-day ranges, Sunday series beginning with the first Sunday in May, summary counts, histograms/timelines, and a personal analysis modal.

\begin{warningbox}[-- Migration Status]
The attendance controller still imports \texttt{firebase/database} directly. The administrator capability protects UI exposure, but the final security boundary therefore still depends on Firebase Security Rules rather than a dedicated Worker attendance API.
\end{warningbox}
\chapter{LInC Archives}
LInC Archives is a delegated-administrator private document workspace. It supports nested folder trees, breadcrumbs, location filtering, multi-file selection, upload-progress/pending UI, verification, download, and deletion.

Folder and file metadata live under \nolinkurl{administration/archives}. Objects are stored in a configured Backblaze B2 S3-compatible bucket. Folder names reject slashes and dot/dot-dot names; file names reject slashes/control characters; declared file size is capped at 5 GB.

Archive upload follows pending-record -> signed upload -> object verification -> ready-record. If signed URL creation fails, the backend attempts to clean the pending metadata. Folder deletion refuses non-empty/nested structures according to archive-domain rules. Access requires Chief or \texttt{manageArchives}.
\chapter{Carousel Management}
The landing carousel is managed in the Administrator Panel and displayed publicly by \texttt{CommunityCarousel}. The admin UI supports enabling/visibility behavior, adding selected images, local pending uploads, image conversion to data URLs, multilingual photo text, ordering/move operations, and deletion.

Unlike archives and NextGen files, carousel data is still represented directly in Realtime Database beneath \nolinkurl{landingPage/carousel}. The public carousel subscribes to that path directly. This makes the carousel one of the remaining direct-Firebase content domains.
\chapter{Tutorial Builder and Player}
The tutorial subsystem is globally available through \texttt{TutorialProvider} and includes a builder section, library, editor modal, player, simulation layer, and target picker. Tutorials have title, description, category, audience, publication state, and ordered steps. A step can carry title/explanation, route, DOM target, requested action, and simulated result.

The builder validates required title/category, requires at least one step, and checks title, explanation, target, and simulated result for each step. Published tutorials can be run using React Joyride-style guided behavior and route/target coordination. Per-user progress tracks last step, completion, completion time, and most recent start.

Tutorial definitions and progress still use direct Realtime Database operations under \nolinkurl{tutorialBuilder/tutorials} and \nolinkurl{tutorialBuilder/progress}.
\chapter{Email and Notification System}
Brevo is the primary server-side transactional email provider. The backend contains specialized builders for assessment messages, identifier messages, public booking requests, calendar notifications, meeting invitations, and People Development notifications. Templates escape user-controlled HTML values and several provide bilingual English/Arabic content.

The shared Brevo service supports ordinary recipient delivery and BCC delivery. Meeting invitations are Pastor-authenticated. Public booking notification is server-generated to a configured fixed recipient. Assessment notifications use fixed configured/repository form recipients and optionally the submitter according to the assessment workflow.

Legacy Gmail/Google Calendar and EmailJS code remains in \texttt{src/services/gmail.ts}; \texttt{App.tsx} still initializes EmailJS and handles an OAuth callback. This code provides Google OAuth token storage, Gmail send, Calendar event/Meet link creation, placeholder link generation, and EmailJS helpers, but it is not the authoritative path for the newer backend-migrated workflows.
\chapter{Localization, Motion, and Accessibility}
The application uses a custom internationalization context with English and Arabic locale switching and RTL/LTR direction. Assessment definitions mix translation keys and embedded bilingual text. Backend email builders and Bezalel prompts also contain bilingual behavior.

The landing page and other interfaces use Motion/AnimatePresence. Reduced-motion preference is respected in prominent landing animations. Branding components include accessibility tests for meaningful versus decorative logo usage. The codebase uses semantic buttons/links, aria labels in several interactive controls, and responsive layouts, although a formal WCAG conformance audit is not present in the supplied repository.
\chapter{Legal, About, and Guide Pages}
Public informational routes include About Us, Privacy Policy, and Terms of Service. The Guide route is Pastor-protected. These are conventional React pages and participate in the shared layout where applicable.

Because the technical stack has changed materially—Gemini, Backblaze B2, server-side Brevo, new NextGen account/file behavior, and expanded Firebase server access—the legal copy should be periodically checked against actual provider/data flows. Release 1.3 already identified policy drift as a risk; the broader v1.4 source audit reinforces that requirement.
