\section{Five Service Pathways Detail}
The Five Service Pathways definition collects ten participant-information fields, five faith/narrative responses, five grouped spiritual-gift dimensions with five ratings each, ten ministry ratings, and six vision/reflection fields. Its calculation pipeline sums gift groups, maps ministry fields to scores, ranks both dimensions, and derives a primary gift, secondary gift, and recommended ministry.

\section{Spiritual Gifts Discovery Detail}
The Spiritual Gifts Discovery definition contains participant information plus fourteen gift groups with three statements each, for a 42-item gift questionnaire. The gift groups are Prophecy, Serving/Helps, Teaching, Encouragement/Exhortation, Giving, Leadership, Mercy/Compassion, Wisdom, Knowledge, Faith, Administration, Evangelism, Pastoring/Shepherding, and Hospitality. The calculation pipeline sums each group, ranks groups, and returns the top three gifts.
\chapter{Backend Architecture}
\section{Worker Composition}
\texttt{backend/src/index.ts} creates one Hono application and mounts domain routers below \nolinkurl{/api/v1}. CORS allows the production site and local Vite origin, accepts \texttt{Content-Type} and \texttt{Authorization}, and permits GET, POST, PATCH, DELETE, and OPTIONS. The preflight max-age is 86,400 seconds.

\section{Authentication}
Firebase ID tokens are verified against Google's secure-token JWK endpoint using RS256, expected project audience, expected Firebase issuer, and time claims. The middleware additionally validates UID and \texttt{auth\_time}, extracts email/email-verified/name/picture/provider fields, and stores both normalized user and raw ID token in Hono context.

\section{Two Database Access Modes}
The backend has two Realtime Database client modes. Some Pastor flows reuse the caller's Firebase ID token, allowing Firebase Security Rules to remain part of authorization. Other server-authoritative domains obtain a short-lived Google OAuth access token from configured Firebase service-account credentials and use an admin-style REST client. The service-account OAuth assertion requests Firebase Database and userinfo-email scopes and caches access tokens until close to expiry.

\section{Validation and Error Boundaries}
Request contracts are generally strict Zod objects. Unknown fields are rejected in security-sensitive routes. Provider/database errors are translated into structured JSON envelopes rather than returning raw credentials or upstream response bodies. Domain modules contain normalization functions so Realtime Database's schema-flexible records are converted before reaching UI/business logic.

\section{Object Storage}
Backblaze integration uses AWS Signature Version 4 through an S3-compatible endpoint. The service creates time-bounded upload/download URLs, inspects objects, deletes objects, validates required bucket/endpoint/region/application-key configuration, and retries inspection of newly uploaded objects to tolerate propagation delay.
