\section{API Design Conventions}
Most application endpoints return \texttt{\{ success: true, data: ... \}} on success and \texttt{\{ success: false, error: \{ code, message, ... \} \}} on failure. Create operations commonly return HTTP 201. Validation failures use 400; missing authentication 401; authorization 403; conflict/duplicate/state errors commonly 409; semantic AI rejection can use 422; missing records 404; provider/database failure commonly 502; missing server configuration commonly 503; and public portal rate limiting uses 429.

\section{Cache-Control}
Sensitive session, People Notes, mission-map, and other authenticated responses set private/no-store behavior. Public form-state and schedule data use short public or no-store behavior depending on endpoint. Maintaining this distinction is important because the Worker returns both public projections and highly confidential data.
