\chapter{Security and Privacy Assessment}
\section{Implemented Controls}
\begin{itemize}
\item Firebase Bearer-token verification on sensitive routers.
\item Server-side exact Pastor authorization for Pastor APIs.
\item Capability-based administrator checks for assessment, archives, and NextGen QA management.
\item Strict Zod validation and rejection of unknown request fields on many write routes.
\item Server-controlled timestamps/status fields and narrow database paths.
\item Privacy-safe public booking projection rather than raw calendar reads.
\item Calendar reservation claiming to reduce double-booking races.
\item Server-held Brevo, service-account, B2, Gemini, and mission-map credentials/data.
\item Backend-controlled email templates and HTML escaping.
\item Signed, short-lived B2 URLs with object verification before ready state.
\item Private/no-store cache policy on sensitive APIs.
\item Question count limit and AI theme moderation in NextGen QA.
\item Identifier portal attempt limiting.
\item Automated backend tests, type-checking, deploy gating, and production contract smoke test.
\end{itemize}

\section{Current Risk Register}
\begin{longtable}{@{}p{0.08\linewidth}p{0.18\linewidth}p{0.18\linewidth}p{0.48\linewidth}@{}}\toprule
\textbf{ID}&\textbf{Severity}&\textbf{Area}&\textbf{Finding}\\\midrule
R1&Critical&People Development notifications&Notification endpoint has no Firebase authentication or Pastor/admin authorization middleware. A caller that can reach it and satisfy schema validation can cause provider sends.\\
R2&High&NextGen files&All file/folder operations are protected only by Firebase authentication; no NextGen membership, Pastor, or administrator authority check is applied.\\
R3&High&Mission map&Current route accepts any authenticated Firebase user with an email; this is broader than repository README wording and may expose private addresses/location notes.\\
R4&Medium&Email test&Temporary \nolinkurl{/api/v1/email/test} route remains publicly mounted and can produce provider traffic to a fixed recipient.\\
R5&High&Residual direct Firebase&Attendance, carousel, tutorials, participant aggregation, and legacy NextGen paths still rely on browser Firebase access and therefore on Security Rules not included in the supplied snapshot.\\
R6&Medium&People Development attachments&Base64 files remain inside Realtime Database, increasing record size and making object lifecycle/authorization less clean than B2 domains.\\
R7&Medium&Legacy integrations&EmailJS and Google OAuth/Gmail/Calendar helpers remain initialized or reachable in source, increasing bundle/maintenance surface and documentation ambiguity.\\
R8&Medium&Identity model&Multiple domain-specific identities complicate access revocation, privacy requests, deduplication, and auditability.\\
R9&Medium&AI abuse/cost&Public Bezalel booking chat has no explicit durable global rate limiter in the reviewed route. Gemini quota errors are handled but abuse prevention is limited.\\
R10&Medium&Admin carousel&Carousel authority is represented in admin profile, but current carousel persistence remains direct from browser, so backend capability allocation is not the complete enforcement boundary.\\
R11&Medium&Attendance&Attendance authority is UI-gated but browser-controlled persistence makes Firebase Rules the decisive boundary.\\
R12&Low/Medium&Legal drift&Privacy/terms copy can become inaccurate as external providers and data categories evolve.\\
R13&Medium&Observability&Wrangler config shows observability commented out; no dedicated audit-log domain is visible in the supplied source.\\
R14&Medium&Revocation&ID-token verification validates JWT claims but does not implement an explicit Firebase revoked-token check in the reviewed middleware.\\
R15&Low/Medium&Repository drift&README and live source already disagree on at least mission-map authorization, so operational docs require release-level synchronization.\\
\bottomrule\end{longtable}

\section{Recommended Closure Order}
First protect or remove the unauthenticated notification/test routes; then tighten NextGen files and mission-map authorization; then complete backend migration for attendance/carousel/tutorials/legacy NextGen; then add durable abuse controls, audit events, security-rule documentation/tests, and a canonical identity strategy.
\chapter{Testing, CI/CD, Deployment, and Operations}
\section{Frontend Workflow}
\nolinkurl{.github/workflows/frontend-build.yml} runs on relevant pushes and pull requests, checks out the repository, installs Node.js 24, runs \texttt{npm ci}, and executes the production frontend build (TypeScript project build plus Vite build).

\section{Backend Verification and Deployment}
\nolinkurl{backend-tests.yml} runs backend tests and TypeScript type checking. For main-branch push or manual dispatch, the production deploy job waits for tests, verifies Cloudflare credentials, runs the Wrangler deploy script, and then executes the production smoke test. Concurrency is serialized for the production backend environment.

\section{Production Smoke}
A separate scheduled workflow runs daily at cron \texttt{17 10 * * *} and can also be manually dispatched. It installs backend dependencies and runs the production NextGen API contract smoke script. The smoke script supports environment overrides for backend URL, attempt count, and retry delay.

\section{Hosting Surfaces}
The frontend is configured for Netlify SPA hosting with \texttt{npm run build} and \nolinkurl{dist}. A wildcard redirect sends client routes to \nolinkurl{/index.html}. The backend is an independently deployed Cloudflare Worker named \texttt{linc-backend}. Frontend and backend contract changes must remain compatible across these separate surfaces.

\section{Netlify Secret Scanning}
The current Netlify configuration excludes source-code paths from secret scanning through \texttt{SECRETS\_SCAN\_OMIT\_PATHS}. This is broader than the earlier single-key omission approach and should be reviewed carefully because excluding source trees can reduce accidental-secret detection.
