\chapter{Complete End-to-End Workflow Reference}
This chapter complements the feature and API catalogs by tracing the principal application behaviors from user action through frontend orchestration, backend or Firebase access, persistence, external providers, and resulting state. It is intentionally redundant with lower-level catalogs where that redundancy helps a maintainer understand a complete transaction without jumping between chapters.

\section{Application Startup, Authentication, and Route Protection}
\textbf{Actors:} anonymous visitor, Firebase-authenticated account, Pastor, administrator. The browser initializes Firebase, internationalization, tutorial context, and React routing. Protected Pastor routes resolve Firebase authentication and then call the backend Pastor-access/session boundary. The backend verifies the Firebase ID token against Google's signing keys and then applies the exact Pastor email allowlist. Administrator access uses the separate administrator session endpoint and the hierarchy stored beneath \nolinkurl{administration/adminHierarchy}. The application still processes legacy Gmail OAuth callback state and initializes EmailJS client configuration during startup, so authentication/bootstrap code contains both current and residual integration paths.

\begin{infobox}[-- Security Boundary]
A successful Firebase login proves account identity only. Pastor authorization and administrator authority are separate decisions. Current NextGen account routes generally require a Firebase account plus an email, while selected management operations additionally require Pastor status or \texttt{manageNextGenQa}.
\end{infobox}

\section{Public Assessment Submission}
The visitor opens \nolinkurl{/assessment}. The frontend obtains the current public assessment states, loads a selected YAML definition, renders fields and rating groups, validates required user input, and presents calculated results. On submission, the backend accepts only a known form identifier, validates the answer structure, recomputes the supported result calculations server-side, records timestamps and metadata, persists the response beneath \nolinkurl{form}, and performs configured notification behavior. This prevents a browser from becoming authoritative for stored scores simply by submitting a precomputed result object.

The two supplied active definitions are \texttt{five-service-pathways} and \texttt{spiritual-gifts-discovery}. Their exact sections, configured item counts, scoring operations, and result outputs are documented in the Assessment Definition Reference chapter.

\section{Assessment Administration and Member Linkage}
An authorized administrator with assessment authority can inspect and change form publication/state controls through backend administration endpoints rather than editing the public definition from the browser. User-linkage operations connect submitted assessment records to People Development member identity. Current source retains a fragmented identity landscape, so the linkage layer remains important: an assessment response, a People Development member, a Firebase account, an attendance person, and a legacy NextGen user are not yet one canonical member record.

\section{Public Booking and Atomic Reservation}
The visitor opens \nolinkurl{/booking}. The frontend requests a date-window projection from the booking API. The backend combines Pastor availability, unavailability, confirmed meetings, pending requests, active calendar reservations, and recurring People Development schedules and returns a sanitized public schedule rather than private meeting details. The visitor selects an exact 30-minute slot, supplies name, email, reason, locale, and the empty anti-bot honeypot, and submits a request.

Before persistence, the Worker revalidates date/time rules and conflicts. It transactionally claims a reservation key so concurrent requests cannot both own the same slot, stores the request as pending, and attempts Pastor notification through Brevo. The public request remains a request; it is not silently converted into a confirmed meeting.

\section{Pastor Meeting-Request Decision}
The Pastor Dashboard receives pending requests through the authenticated calendar snapshot. On accept, the backend verifies Pastor authorization, reloads current scheduling state, rechecks conflicts, creates the confirmed meeting, updates request state, reconciles reservation state, and sends requester notification. On reject, it updates request state and sends the corresponding communication. The public requester never receives the Pastor's private calendar contents.

\section{Pastor Meeting Creation, Editing, and Participant Invitations}
Pastor can create an internal meeting/event independently of a public request. The validated meeting contract includes title, description, date, start/end time, location, meeting type, optional meeting link, and participant identifiers. Calendar mutations are backend-owned and the client refreshes the shared snapshot after mutation. Participant invitation delivery is a separate backend Brevo workflow: the browser submits structured meeting/recipient data with a Firebase Bearer token, the Worker builds localized controlled HTML/plain-text email, and the provider credential remains server-side.

\section{Pastor Availability and Unavailability}
Availability and unavailability records are managed through dedicated authenticated calendar routes. The UI supports generated windows as well as record-level edits/deletions. These records feed both the Pastor calendar and the privacy-safe public schedule. Because booking conflict logic reads them again at request/decision time, the public schedule display is not the only defense against invalid slot claims.

\section{Bezalel Pastor Calendar Assistance}
Pastor opens Bezalel from the Pastor workspace and writes a natural-language calendar instruction. The Worker sends a controlled baseline prompt plus a sanitized calendar context to Gemini. The allowed structured action set is deliberately narrow: \texttt{none}, \texttt{open\_availability}, \texttt{block\_time}, \texttt{delete\_availability}, \texttt{delete\_unavailability}, \texttt{accept\_request}, or \texttt{reject\_request}. Bezalel does not directly mutate Firebase from the model call. The model returns a structured action proposal; the frontend executes the corresponding existing calendar API operation, preserving normal validation/authorization.

\begin{warningbox}[-- AI Control Model]
The important control is not that the model is ``trusted.'' The model is constrained to a schema and the application owns execution. Calendar mutation APIs remain authoritative and must continue to validate identity, Pastor authority, input, and conflicts independently of the generated action.
\end{warningbox}

\section{Bezalel Public Booking Assistance}
The public booking assistant operates on the sanitized public schedule rather than the Pastor's private calendar. It can answer booking questions, collect missing booking information, and move the conversation toward a ready-to-book state. The final booking still goes through the normal booking request contract and conflict controls; the assistant is not a bypass around the booking API.

\section{People Development Pastor Management}
The Pastor snapshot normalizes members, assignments, personal notes, and recurring schedules from \nolinkurl{peopleDevelopment}. Pastor can assign a member to a ministry group, create assignments/posts targeted to one or more groups, attach files, record member-specific strength/weakness notes, and create recurring group/shared meeting schedules. Group changes use backend-controlled multi-path updates so relevant linkage information in source assessment records stays synchronized with the People Development member.

Assignment attachments remain embedded as Base64 records in Realtime Database in the reviewed source, with explicit file-count/size validation. This differs from LInC Archives and NextGen Files, which use Backblaze B2 object storage.

\section{Congregation Group Notes Portal}
The participant opens \nolinkurl{/group-notes} and supplies the ministry identifier expected by the public portal. The backend applies rate limiting, resolves the matching participant/member identity, determines group membership, and returns only assignments and active recurring schedules relevant to that profile/group. The portal is intentionally identifier-based rather than Firebase-account based in the current implementation, so identifier secrecy, rate limiting, and returned-data minimization matter.

\section{People Development Notification Delivery}
The current notification route accepts a validated People Development notification payload and sends BCC mail through Brevo. Unlike the Pastor management routes, the reviewed route is not wrapped in Firebase authentication middleware. This is documented as a current implementation fact and a critical remediation item, not as an intended public feature.

\section{Confidential People Notes}
Pastor opens \nolinkurl{/pastor/people-notes}. Every API operation requires Firebase authentication and Pastor authorization. Pastor can create a person record, add development items, append comments, update follow-up dates, and delete person/item/comment records. Server-owned timestamps are updated with mutations. Responses are treated as non-cacheable/private. This domain is separate from People Development personal notes and should remain a high-sensitivity authorization boundary.

\section{NextGen Account Session}
The active \nolinkurl{/nextgen-activities} experience is Firebase email/password account based. After sign-in, the client obtains NextGen session identity and Pastor metadata and can load the account-visible QA sessions, files interface, and mission map. This differs materially from the legacy four-character identifier activities still present in source but no longer mounted as the primary NextGen route.

\section{NextGen QA Session and Question Submission}
An authenticated member lists non-draft QA sessions and opens an active session. Before accepting a question, the backend enforces session state and an account question limit. It then invokes Bezalel/Gemini to compare the question against the session theme. The model response is schema-validated; an off-theme decision is returned as a semantic rejection, while an accepted question is stored in the session data. The one-time legacy migration creates a closed historical session for earlier QA data when required.

\section{NextGen Voting}
Voting is tied to the authenticated account/email rather than a freely supplied short identifier. The backend maintains one current vote per account/question and supports changing the vote. Member-facing data is intentionally narrower than management data; management counts and participant verification states are exposed only to the appropriate management boundary.

\section{NextGen QA Management}
A Pastor or administrator with \texttt{manageNextGenQa} can create/edit session title, description, status, and theme; select questions for discussion; and manage participant verification/discarded state. Theme translation can invoke Gemini when the source theme changes. Management endpoints use a shared authorization helper so Pastor authority and delegated QA authority remain separate from ordinary member access.

\section{NextGen File Upload, Completion, Download, and Delete}
The browser requests a signed upload URL, uploads the object directly to Backblaze B2, and then calls the completion endpoint. The backend inspects the object before marking metadata ready. Downloads similarly use signed private URLs. Folder/file metadata is persisted beneath \nolinkurl{nextGenPortal/files}. In the reviewed source, the route-level authorization requirement is only a Firebase-authenticated account; therefore upload, folder creation/deletion, file listing, signed download, and deletion are broader than a typical member-only/private-content policy would imply.

\section{Montreal Mission Map}
The frontend requests mission-map location/connection data from the Worker, then uses Google Maps/browser routing facilities for map display and route details. The sensitive location graph is stored in the \texttt{NEXTGEN\_MISSION\_MAP\_DATA} Worker secret rather than compiled into the frontend. The current route checks for a valid Firebase user carrying an email. Repository prose that describes one specific mission account is therefore stricter than the code and should not be treated as the enforced rule.

\section{Administrator Onboarding and Authority Allocation}
A Firebase-authenticated account opening \nolinkurl{/administrator} calls the administrator session API. When the hierarchy contains no Chief, the exact Pastor account can bootstrap the Chief. Other new accounts are represented as pending/non-authoritative until the Chief activates them. The Chief can allocate the five current authority booleans or suspend an administrator. Backend-protected domains check the relevant authority rather than trusting that a UI tab was hidden.

\section{LInC Archives File Lifecycle}
An authorized Chief or \texttt{manageArchives} administrator can create nested folder metadata, request a B2 upload URL, upload directly to B2, complete/verify the object, obtain a private signed download URL, and delete files. The backend prevents deletion of a non-empty folder and keeps Firebase metadata separate from B2 object bytes. File size is bounded by the archive contract and server inspection is used before a pending file is promoted to ready.

\section{Attendance Management}
Attendance remains a direct-client Firebase domain in the reviewed source. The administrator UI maintains people, optional photos/contact details, Sunday attendance records, analytics, and person-level analysis under \nolinkurl{attendance/people}. Access is gated by the administrator UI/capability experience, but no equivalent Worker persistence boundary was found for attendance. Consequently, effective security still depends on Firebase database rules that were not supplied in this snapshot.

\section{Landing Carousel Management}
Public \texttt{CommunityCarousel} subscribes directly to \nolinkurl{landingPage/carousel}. The administrator carousel UI also writes through client-side Firebase. Although \texttt{manageCarousel} exists in the administrator authority model, the persistence operation is not yet routed through a Worker endpoint that can independently enforce that capability. This makes Firebase Rules part of the actual enforcement boundary.

\section{Tutorial Authoring, Simulation, Playback, and Progress}
The tutorial subsystem supports builder/editor screens, ordered steps, route/target/action metadata, simulation overlays, target selection, tutorial playback, and per-user progress. Tutorial definitions and progress are stored directly through Firebase helper code beneath \nolinkurl{tutorialBuilder/tutorials} and \nolinkurl{tutorialBuilder/progress}. The tutorial context is mounted at application level so tutorials can span routes and identify UI targets.

\section{Legacy Integrations and Residual Paths}
The repository retains EmailJS startup/configuration, Gmail/Google OAuth code, legacy NextGen Firebase modules, and an older \texttt{BookMeeting} component in addition to the newer backend-centered paths. Package dependencies also include OpenAI and \texttt{@google/genai} without active imports in the reviewed source. These artifacts are not assumed to be active merely because they exist; they are cataloged because they affect maintenance, bundle surface, migration decisions, and future cleanup.

\chapter{Enterprise Target Architecture and Modernization Reference}
This chapter preserves the architecture/governance purpose of the earlier documentation while reclassifying work that the current source has already implemented. It is a target-state reference: statements using ``shall'' or ``should'' describe desired controls, not necessarily current behavior.

\section{Architecture Principles}
\begin{enumerate}
\item \textbf{Server authority for sensitive mutations.} Confidential data, permissions, bookings, notifications, AI orchestration, and private files should cross an authenticated backend boundary.
\item \textbf{Least privilege.} Firebase authentication, Pastor authority, Chief status, and delegated capabilities are distinct concepts and should remain independently enforced.
\item \textbf{Public projections instead of public databases.} Anonymous features receive sanitized data shaped for the feature rather than broad database branches.
\item \textbf{Deterministic domain logic outside UI components.} Validation, scoring, conflict detection, authorization, and persistence rules belong in testable domain/server modules.
\item \textbf{Explicit data classification.} Public, internal, confidential, highly confidential, and secret data require different storage, caching, logging, and access rules.
\item \textbf{Incremental migration.} The existing product remains usable while direct-client Firebase domains are progressively moved behind APIs.
\item \textbf{Provider isolation.} Brevo, B2, Firebase Admin REST access, Google Maps, and Gemini should be hidden behind focused adapters rather than spread through page components.
\item \textbf{Operational evidence.} CI, smoke tests, backups, audit logs, monitoring, and runbooks are part of production completeness, not optional documentation afterthoughts.
\end{enumerate}

\section{Current Target Stack}
The current code has validated a pragmatic stack: React/TypeScript/Vite on Netlify for the browser, Firebase Authentication for identity, Cloudflare Workers + Hono + Zod for API/orchestration, Firebase Realtime Database for current structured persistence, Backblaze B2 for private object storage, Brevo for transactional mail, and Gemini for bounded AI features. PostgreSQL remains an optional later migration rather than a prerequisite for finishing the server boundary. A database change should be driven by relational/transactional needs and migration benefit, not architecture aesthetics alone.

\section{Target Request Path}
For sensitive features, the preferred path is:
\begin{center}
\textbf{Browser $\rightarrow$ Firebase identity token $\rightarrow$ Worker authorization/validation $\rightarrow$ domain service $\rightarrow$ database/object/provider}
\end{center}
Public endpoints are allowed when the feature itself is public, but they must expose feature-specific projections, validate input, apply abuse controls, and never rely on hidden UI state as authorization.

\section{Capability and Role Model}
The current source has implemented Chief plus five delegated authority booleans, while Pastor remains a separate exact-email authority. The longer-term capability model should become action-oriented rather than UI-oriented. Recommended capability namespaces include:
\begin{multicols}{2}\begin{itemize}
\item \texttt{assessment.forms.manage}
\item \texttt{assessment.responses.link}
\item \texttt{carousel.manage}
\item \texttt{attendance.manage}
\item \texttt{archives.manage}
\item \texttt{nextgen.qa.manage}
\item \texttt{nextgen.files.manage}
\item \texttt{booking.requests.decide}
\item \texttt{calendar.availability.manage}
\item \texttt{calendar.meetings.manage}
\item \texttt{people-development.manage}
\item \texttt{people-notes.manage}
\item \texttt{tutorials.manage}
\item \texttt{admin.authority.allocate}
\end{itemize}\end{multicols}
Pastoral care decisions and highly confidential pastoral records should remain non-delegable by ordinary technical administrators unless an explicit ministry-governance decision creates a separate pastoral role.

\section{Canonical Identity Target}
The current repository contains several identities: Firebase account UID/email, administrator UID, assessment response identifier, People Development member key/identifier, attendance person key, and legacy NextGen IDs. The target model should introduce a stable member/person identity with explicit links to authentication accounts and feature records. Feature-specific confidential data should remain in separate domains rather than becoming one giant member document.

A canonical identity migration should preserve historical identifiers and support deterministic reconciliation. It should not rewrite old records in place without an audit/reversal plan.

