\section{API Standards}
Every production endpoint should document method/path, authentication, authorization/capability, request schema, response schema, error semantics, rate limits, cache policy, data classification, persistence side effects, provider side effects, and audit requirements. Zod strict objects should remain the default for mutation contracts. Error responses should not leak provider credentials, raw service-account assertions, secret configuration, or full upstream bodies.

An OpenAPI description is recommended for the growing API surface. With 81 cataloged endpoints, hand-maintained frontend types alone become increasingly difficult to keep synchronized.

\section{Transaction and Idempotency Standards}
Booking conflict/reservation logic already demonstrates transaction-aware design. Similar discipline should be applied to any workflow where duplicate submission matters: archive/file completion, email retry, invitation fan-out, attendance correction imports, NextGen votes, and administrator authority changes. Where external providers are involved, an idempotency key or durable job/event record should separate ``domain transaction succeeded'' from ``notification/provider delivery succeeded.''

\section{Security Architecture}
The target security model includes Firebase identity verification, explicit authorization, server-side validation, provider-secret isolation, abuse/rate controls, private object URLs, least-privilege service credentials, secure cache headers, structured logs without sensitive payloads, and periodic access review. Firebase Rules remain defense-in-depth even after server migration and are the primary enforcement mechanism for any direct-client domain that remains.

Revoked-token checking should be considered for high-sensitivity operations. The current Worker validates signed token claims but the reviewed authentication implementation does not perform an explicit Firebase revoked-token lookup.

\section{Privacy and Data Governance}
Data categories should have written retention and correction/deletion rules. Highly confidential pastoral notes require the strongest access controls and the least logging of content. Participant survey/QA claims must accurately describe whether data is anonymous, pseudonymous, or account-linked. Public legal text should name actual providers and data flows in the deployed architecture; current source and provider usage have evolved faster than the policy prose.

\section{Audit Logging Target}
A durable audit event should record security-relevant mutations without copying confidential content unnecessarily. Recommended fields include event ID, timestamp, actor UID, actor email or stable actor key, authorization source, capability, action, domain, target record ID, result, request correlation ID, and non-sensitive change metadata. Audit data should be append-only to ordinary application actors and covered by retention policy.

\section{Observability and Incident Response}
The Worker should emit structured request/error metrics and domain/provider failure counts. Monitoring should cover authentication failures, authorization denials, public endpoint rate-limit events, booking conflicts, Brevo failures, B2 completion failures, Gemini failures/429s, and smoke-test health. The runbook should define incident severity, containment, credential rotation, privacy assessment, recovery, and post-incident review.

\section{Backup and Recovery}
Realtime Database backups/exports and B2 object retention/versioning must be coordinated so metadata and objects can be restored consistently. Recovery documentation should specify responsible operator, backup schedule, retention, restore test cadence, and recovery-time/recovery-point objectives appropriate to ministry operations. Backups containing People Notes or pastoral data must be treated as highly confidential copies, not as ordinary developer artifacts.

\section{Frontend Modernization Direction}
The frontend should continue moving from page-owned persistence toward typed domain clients and hooks. Large components should compose smaller feature modules rather than owning authentication, database paths, business logic, provider calls, and presentation together. Direct Firebase imports should be treated as an explicit migration list; this release provides that list in Appendix F.

Localization should preserve English/Arabic directionality and keep feature-specific assessment text authoritative in versioned form definitions. Accessibility work should cover keyboard interaction, labels, focus state, reduced motion, semantic headings, readable contrast, and screen-reader behavior for dialogs/interactive maps.

\section{Backend Module Design}
The current Worker already demonstrates useful separation: routes, schemas, security/auth helpers, provider services, data access, AI prompt/service code, and domain-specific modules. Future work should preserve that separation and avoid turning \nolinkurl{backend/src/index.ts} into a monolithic application service. Domain modules should own domain rules; infrastructure modules should own Firebase/B2/Brevo/Gemini mechanics.

\section{Testing Strategy}
The 167 cataloged named tests provide substantial regression coverage, especially for backend contracts. The target test strategy remains layered:
\begin{itemize}
\item pure unit tests for calculations, normalizers, date/slot logic, prompt schema handling, and authorization helpers;
\item route tests for authentication, authorization, validation, success, conflict, not-found, provider failure, and configuration failure;
\item integration tests around Firebase REST transactions and object-storage lifecycle where deterministic fixtures are practical;
\item frontend behavior tests for complex administrator/Pastor workflows;
\item production smoke tests limited to safe read/contract checks;
\item migration reconciliation tests before deleting legacy/direct-client paths.
\end{itemize}
A feature should not be considered complete merely because the happy-path UI works.

\section{CI/CD and Environment Model}
The repository now builds the frontend in GitHub Actions, tests and type-checks the backend, deploys the Worker from the main branch after successful backend validation, runs a post-deploy smoke test, and runs a scheduled daily production NextGen smoke. This supersedes the v1.2 statement that Worker deployment was manual only.

A mature environment model should clearly separate local, test/preview, staging, and production credentials/data. Production service-account/Brevo/B2/Gemini secrets should never be available to frontend build contexts. Netlify secret-scan exclusions should be kept as narrow as practical; broad source exclusions reduce the value of automated secret detection.

\section{Documentation and Governance Set}
The repository should ultimately carry this reference plus operational documents: developer setup, environment-variable catalog, API/OpenAPI specification, data dictionary/ERDs, authorization matrix, deployment/runbook, backup/restore procedure, incident response, migration logs, architecture decision records, provider inventory, privacy data-flow register, and release notes. Each release should update the source counters, endpoints, residual Firebase list, risk register, and implementation-status matrix from the source tree rather than copying old assumptions.

