\section{Immediate Remediation Queue}
\begin{enumerate}
\item Require appropriate authentication/authorization for \nolinkurl{/api/v1/people-development/notifications}.
\item Replace broad NextGen file access with an explicit audience/capability/ownership rule and tests.
\item Make mission-map code enforce the intended account/audience instead of relying on README prose.
\item Remove or lock the temporary public Brevo test endpoint in production.
\item Obtain and review production Firebase Security Rules because direct-client domains depend on them.
\item Move attendance and carousel writes behind backend capability checks or prove equivalent Rules enforcement.
\item Move People Development binary attachments from Base64 RTDB storage to private object storage when attachment volume warrants it.
\item Remove or isolate unused EmailJS/Gmail/legacy NextGen paths after confirming no production dependency.
\item Add durable audit events and structured observability for sensitive mutations/provider actions.
\item Bring privacy/terms/provider documentation into alignment with current Firebase, Cloudflare, Brevo, B2, Gemini, and Google Maps data flows.
\end{enumerate}

\section{Medium-Term Engineering Queue}
Complete canonical identity design; formalize OpenAPI and generated/validated frontend contracts; implement robust per-principal/public rate limiting; establish environment/staging strategy; define storage/file scanning policy; automate database backup/restore verification; improve map/file authorization granularity; and continue reducing direct Firebase imports until every intentionally client-owned path has reviewed Rules and a documented reason to remain client-owned.

\chapter{Enterprise Readiness and Acceptance Criteria}
\section{Architecture Acceptance}
The application is architecturally ready for controlled transfer when each sensitive domain has an explicit owner, a documented trust boundary, a tested persistence path, and no accidental provider secret in browser code. Direct Firebase access that remains must be intentional, listed, and backed by reviewed Rules.

\section{Security Acceptance}
Production security acceptance requires reviewed Firebase Rules; elimination/locking of unintended public mutation/provider endpoints; least-privilege NextGen file/map access; secret inventory/rotation procedure; audited high-sensitivity changes; rate/abuse controls for public AI and identifier portals; tested authorization denials; and a process for dependency/security updates.

\section{Data Acceptance}
Every active root/object namespace must have an owner, schema/normalization description, retention classification, backup plan, and migration policy. Identity links must be reconcilable. B2 metadata and object records must be restorable together. Pastoral/highly confidential records require explicit deletion/correction and access-governance procedures.

\section{Product Acceptance}
Public, participant, Pastor, and administrator workflows should have documented acceptance tests in both desktop and mobile layouts where applicable, including English/Arabic presentation, error states, empty states, loading states, and denied-access behavior. AI-assisted flows must degrade safely when Gemini is unavailable or rejects a request.

\section{Operational Acceptance}
A competent maintainer should be able to build frontend/backend, configure environments, run tests, deploy both hosting surfaces, verify smoke health, rotate provider credentials, restore backups, diagnose common provider/database failures, and identify the owner of each domain without relying on undocumented knowledge from the original developer.

\section{Commercial/Transfer Acceptance}
For external handoff or licensing, the repository should include clear IP ownership, dependency/license inventory, deployment costs/providers, data-processing/provider list, environment setup, architecture decisions, known-risk register, current roadmap, and support expectations. Estimates of monetary value should be maintained separately from engineering truth so commercial assumptions do not become architecture requirements.

\chapter{Open Verification Items and Documentation Boundaries}
The source audit is intentionally explicit about artifacts that were not available or facts that cannot be proven from static code alone.
\begin{enumerate}
\item Production Firebase Realtime Database Security Rules were not present in the supplied snapshot; therefore actual direct-client read/write enforcement for attendance, carousel, tutorials, and residual NextGen code cannot be certified from this repository alone.
\item Production Cloudflare secret values, Netlify environment values, Firebase console settings, Google Maps restrictions, Brevo account configuration, B2 bucket policy, and Gemini account/quota policy are external deployment state and were not inferred from checked-in names.
\item Static source proves that CI workflows are defined, not that every historical workflow run succeeded. The scheduled smoke workflow proves intended automation, not continuous production health.
\item The source tree contains legacy/unused-looking modules and dependencies. A module is classified as residual when active route/import tracing does not establish it as the current primary path; removal still requires production regression verification.
\item Database ERDs are repository documentation and may contain conceptual entities/fields broader than current raw Firebase records. The persistence chapter therefore distinguishes observed roots from ERD-derived conceptual structure.
\item The named-function catalog is static-source inventory. Anonymous inline closures and framework-generated functions are not represented as separate named entries unless they matched the catalog extraction rules.
\item Provider and legal/privacy obligations require organizational/legal review beyond source inspection. This document identifies technical data flows but is not legal advice.
\end{enumerate}

\begin{notebox}[-- Documentation Maintenance Rule]
For the next release, re-run the source inventory and compare route tables, endpoint mounts, environment names, Firebase imports, dependencies, test names, ERDs, and security middleware against this release. Do not carry a feature status forward merely because it was documented here; current source and deployment evidence must remain authoritative.
\end{notebox}

